    "cost_closing_html":  "About \u003cstrong\u003e${{cost_ps}} a bowl\u003c/strong\u003e (at everyday cost). Cabbage is the most underrated vegetable in the store on a cost per pound basis, and here it is a headliner.",
    "shop_smart":  [
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eCabbage is the cheapest filling vegetable you can buy.\u003c/strong\u003e One head is a couple dollars, keeps for weeks in the crisper, and cooks down into something that eats like a lot more than it cost.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eCanned diced tomatoes carry this pot.\u003c/strong\u003e Watch for the case sale, buy a stack, and you have the base for this and half a dozen other recipes on the site.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eGround turkey freezes flat.\u003c/strong\u003e Buy the larger pack, press it into one pound sheets in freezer bags, and it thaws in an hour instead of overnight.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eCook the rice separately, always.\u003c/strong\u003e Rice simmered in the soup keeps drinking broth in the fridge and turns day four into paste. Cooked apart, it stays rice."
                   ],
    "make_it":  [
                    "Weigh your empty mixing pot and write the number down for portioning later.",
                    "Cook the rice separately per the package and set it aside covered.",
                    "Brown the ground turkey in olive oil in your big pot over medium-high heat, breaking it up, until it is cooked through.",
                    "Add the diced onion, celery and carrots and cook until they start to soften, then stir in the garlic for the last minute.",
                    "Core and chop the cabbage into ribbons and pile it into the pot. It will look like too much. Give it five minutes and it will not be.",
                    "Pour in the diced tomatoes with their juice and the chicken broth, add the dried oregano, Italian seasoning and dried thyme, and bring it to a simmer.",
                    "Simmer 25 to 30 minutes until the cabbage is fully tender, then season with salt and black pepper and stir in the chopped fresh basil off the heat."
                ],
